title the roster that held out one room -- constel's 130 compound asserts split, and the second roster deleted rather than widened
prompt Open as SILENCE under the INNER LOOP Standing block -- roster cold then hot, reds first as standfast, QA on touch, council rota row N mod 5, prose witnesses on prose sends, stamp-and-name, ASCII-first, READ SCOPE -- then pick up the running thread and take the infrastructure lane.
think ListAgents named grain-38 at the open and the checkout proved it: 44 staged files, a glow/gen/ letter fold plus the REDS pin mid-write. I sent the peer the collision surface before a byte moved -- glow/ and the construction/ pins are theirs today -- and took a lap with zero path overlap. The peer committed 2007d1321 mid-lap and freed the ledger, which is why the closure below could be written into its own row rather than only into a commit body.
think The rota read row 0, Aether/Jupiter, why the work exists, sense of hearing: the threshold, follow-our-compass cardinal, the Lindy effect fixed, the grain-and-the-crossing dual. N is git rev-list --count HEAD at 3610 and 3610 mod 5 is 0; the previous Silence lap read row 2, so no repeat and no hand-advance. The dual seat governed the lap in one sentence -- a guard that cannot red guards nothing -- and its converse, learned here, is that a guard reddening on what is right is a guard someone turns off.
think The mechanism: constel/'s 130 compound asserts split one assert per conjunct by a depth- and string-aware pass, each trailing // comment lifted to its own line above the group it describes. Two of the 130 refused the split for opposite reasons and both were real findings. constel/reconfig.rye:149 asserts self.reached == ((A) and (B)), where the conjunction is the RIGHT OPERAND of a comparison -- splitting it would have changed the claim from "reached equals the joint rule" to "reached equals A, and separately B" -- so the conjunction is named as a joint_holds const outside the assert and the assert became a plain equality. constel/vote.rye:155 was never a violation at all.
think vote.rye:155 is the one worth keeping. tame_style_compound_assert.sh was written a day earlier because a grep cannot tell a conjunction from a quoted string, and it blanks quoted content before looking. It skipped a FULL comment line and left a TRAILING one standing as code, so `assert(cand_grants == 5); // every voter grants ... (equal) and the two behind (candidate ahead)` read as a violation on the strength of English prose. That is the same fault the helper exists to fix, one layer over: it learned to ignore literals and had not learned to ignore comments. It strips the comment after the quotes now -- quotes first, so a // inside a literal cannot truncate real code.
obs find_rye_compound is DELETED rather than widened. Adding constel to it would have made it byte-identical to find_rye, and two rosters that must agree are one roster plus a bug waiting for the day they stop agreeing -- this pass proved that by having to edit both. The compound ban now reads the same rooms as the other seven.
obs No clean roster reading this lap, said plainly rather than claimed. A cold standing_equipment_run.sh over a peer's dirty index refuses under run_verdict=lap_unclosed, and --hot would claim their 44 staged paths as my round's, which would be false. That is %295's territory -- a standing roster is one host's promise, and it is also one WRITER's -- not a new red. The module gates ran instead, scoped and green.
obs The ASCII ratchet in constel is named rather than swept: 302 non-ASCII characters over 31 files, 217 of them em-dashes and 43 section signs in Raft citations. My edit left the count unchanged at 276 lines before and after, so nothing was made worse; the sweep is its own lap because a 302-character normalization inside a 129-assert split makes one commit two commits wearing one hat.
